section_text:
Save as otherwise expressly provided in this Act:

(a) any text, rule or interpretation of Hindu law or any custom or usage as part of that law in force immediately before the commencement of this Act shall cease to have effect with respect to any matter for which provision is made in this Act;

(b) any other law in force immediately before the commencement of this Act shall cease to have effect in so far as it is inconsistent with any of the provisions contained in this Act.

In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires:
(a) 'agnate' — two persons are said to be agnates of each other if they are related by blood or adoption wholly through males;
(b) 'cognate' — if they are related by blood or adoption but not wholly through males;
(c) 'intestate' — a person is deemed to die intestate in respect of property of which he or she has not made a testamentary disposition capable of taking effect.

The property of a male Hindu dying intestate shall devolve:
(a) Firstly, upon the heirs in Class I of the Schedule (Son, Daughter, Widow, Mother, etc.);
(b) Secondly, if no Class I heir, then upon the heirs in Class II;
(c) Thirdly, if no Class II heir, then upon the agnates;
(d) Lastly, if no agnate, then upon the cognates.

The property of a female Hindu dying intestate shall devolve:
(a) firstly, upon the sons and daughters and the husband;
(b) secondly, upon the heirs of the husband;
(c) thirdly, upon the mother and father; (d) fourthly, upon the heirs of the father; (e) lastly, upon the heirs of the mother.

Exception: Property inherited from her father/mother devolves upon the heirs of the father in the absence of her own children.

The natural guardian has power to do all acts which are necessary or reasonable for the benefit of the minor. However, previous permission of the court is mandatory for:
(a) mortgage, charge, sale or gift of minor's immovable property;
(b) lease of property for more than 5 years.
